# Architecture Rules — `mcp/`

This project follows **DDD** (Domain-Driven Design) + **Hexagonal Architecture** (Ports & Adapters) with manual constructor injection. The structure mirrors the api repo, adapted for TypeScript.

## Layers

```
src/
├── domain/          # Inner core — depends on nothing outward
│   ├── value-objects/
│   ├── ports/       # interfaces (1 file = 1 port)
│   └── services/    # pure decision policies (no I/O)
├── application/     # use cases
│   ├── tools/       # MCP tool definitions (1 file = 1 tool)
│   └── tool-registry.ts
├── infrastructure/  # adapters implementing ports
│   ├── api/
│   ├── logging/
│   ├── session/
│   ├── rate-limit/
│   ├── clock/
│   └── error-reporting/
├── presentation/    # transports — composition roots
│   ├── stdio/
│   └── http/
├── shared/          # generic utilities (Result, config schema)
└── bin.ts           # CLI entry; dispatches to a bootstrap
```

## Dependency direction (enforced by `dependency-cruiser`)

```
presentation ──→ application ──→ domain
                                ↑
infrastructure ─────────────────┘   (implements ports)

shared ←── used by anyone, depends on nothing
```

Concretely:

- `domain/` imports from nowhere outward. Defines ports; doesn't know what implements them.
- `application/` imports from `domain/` only. Tools depend on `ApiGateway`, `Logger`, `Clock`, etc. — never on concrete classes.
- `infrastructure/` implements `domain/ports/*`. Wires fetch/pino/etc. to satisfy a port. Stays out of `application/`.
- `presentation/` wires everything together. The `*-bootstrap.ts` files are the **only** place `new HttpApiGateway(...)` exists.
- `shared/` is generic utilities (`Result`, env-schema). No domain types live here.

ESLint enforces this with `no-restricted-imports` patterns. dependency-cruiser enforces it again with `mcp/.dependency-cruiser.cjs`. Two locks beats one.

## Ports & Adapters

Every external interaction (HTTP, logging, time, randomness) goes through a port:

| Port              | Adapter                       | Purpose                                            |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| `ApiGateway`      | `HttpApiGateway`              | Calls Kaminari Ad `/api/v1` via undici             |
| `Logger`          | `PinoLogger`                  | Structured logs with redaction                     |
| `Clock`           | `SystemClock`                 | `now(): Date` — mocked in tests                    |
| `RateLimiter`     | `LeakyBucketRateLimiter`      | Per-tenant rate limit (HTTP mode)                  |
| `SessionStore`    | `InMemorySessionStore`        | Mcp-Session-Id <-> bearer-hash binding             |
| `ErrorReporter`   | `NoopErrorReporter`           | OSS default. Operators may swap in Sentry locally. |

## Tools are use cases

A tool corresponds to exactly one outbound API call. Multi-step orchestration belongs in `domain/services/`, with the tool calling the service. If your tool handler grows past 30 lines, that's a smell — extract.

```ts
// application/tools/scans/list-scans.tool.ts
export const listScansTool: Tool<ListScansInput, ListScansOutput> = {
  name: "list_scans",
  description: "List scans for the caller's organization with filters.",
  inputSchema: ListScansInputSchema,
  handler: async (input, ctx) => {
    const result = await ctx.api.listScans(input);
    return result.map(formatAsToolOutput);
  },
};
```

Note:

- No `throw` — `handler` returns `Result<Output, ToolError>` (the type is enforced by the `Tool<I,O>` interface).
- No `ctx.api.listScans(input as any)` — strict typing all the way through.
- The `formatAsToolOutput` helper is in the same file unless it's reused by 3+ tools.

## Composition roots

Two composition roots, one per transport:

- `presentation/stdio/stdio-bootstrap.ts` — takes `Config`, returns a ready `McpServer`. Used by `bin.ts --transport=stdio`.
- `presentation/http/http-bootstrap.ts` — takes `Config`, returns a `http.Server`. Builds long-lived stores (session, rate-limit); per-request creates a fresh `HttpApiGateway` from the incoming Bearer.

Composition roots are the ONLY place where `new ConcreteAdapter(...)` is allowed. Every other file works with port interfaces.

There is NO DI framework. Manual constructor injection. At this scale, a DI framework is more cognitive overhead than it saves.

## Tenant isolation governs the HTTP composition root

The HTTP bootstrap MUST follow every rule in [`mcp-tenant-isolation.mdc`](.cursor/rules/mcp-tenant-isolation.mdc). In particular:

- The long-lived top-level singletons (`SessionStore`, `RateLimiter`, `Logger` parent) hold **no** tenant data — only by-Bearer-hash entries.
- The per-request `ToolContext` (containing the `HttpApiGateway` instance bound to the request's Bearer) is created inside the request handler and lives only in that handler's closure.

The bootstrap MUST `assert(!config.stdioApiKey, ...)` on startup in HTTP mode. The `KAMINARI_AD_API_KEY` env var is a stdio-only fallback; having it set in HTTP mode is an operator error.

## What `shared/` is for

- `Result<T, E>` re-export and helpers
- `Config` zod schema + parser for env vars
- Branded type helpers
- Generic timing utilities

What `shared/` is NOT for: anything domain-specific. If it knows about Bearer, Scan, Campaign — it belongs in `domain/`.
